Source: isa-check
Section: utils
Priority: optional
Maintainer: Ubuntu Developers <ubuntu-devel-discuss@lists.ubuntu.com>
Build-Depends: mawk | awk, binutils, debhelper-compat (= 13), dpkg (>= 1.22.21ubuntu4),
 dpkg-dev (>= 1.22.21ubuntu4), meson (>= 0.63), ninja-build
Standards-Version: 4.7.0
Rules-Requires-Root: no

Package: isa-check
Architecture: amd64
Depends: dpkg (>= 1.22.21ubuntu4), ${misc:Depends}, ${shlibs:Depends}
Description: list and probe compatible processor architecture variants
 isa-check reads dpkg's varianttable and checks every processor on the running
 system. With no arguments it lists all compatible variants in table order.
 With a variant argument it reports whether that variant is supported and
 returns a script-friendly status, including missing CPU features on failure.

Package: isa-preflight
Architecture: any
Depends: ${misc:Depends}
Built-Using: ${static:Built-Using}
Description: verify CPU features or chain-load initramfs userspace
 isa-preflight is a static executable intended to run as /init before any
 dynamic loader, shell, or init system. When DEB_HOST_ARCH_VARIANT is set, it
 checks every processor against a generated feature list, reports missing CPU
 features, and halts on failure. Non-variant builds provide a no-op chainloader
 so image builders can use a uniform dependency and handoff.
 .
 Variant requirements are resolved from dpkg's varianttable at build time.
 On success the configured successor is executed with the original arguments
 and environment. The initrd builder should provide its path through next-init.
